      "reasoning": "I'll need to update this probe (it asserts exactly 1 injection). After B/C/D, the audit list will have many rows (all of the static sections + providers). But careful: providers that need conversation context (persona, skills, topics) - `auditInjections` calls providers with `{conversationId}` only (string param). persona needs `input.read.snapshot(ChatKinds)` which is async and requires durable read. That's a problem: the provider signature is sync `(ctx) => ...`.\n\nSo how to take over `persona`? The section render receives `input` with `input.read` (async snapshot). We can precompute the async context in the render and pass to collectInjections? But then provider must be sync and receive that.\n\nLook at InjectionContext: currently only conversationId. I can extend it with optional fields, e.g. `kinds?: ...` and `partnerId?: ...`. But provider is sync, so persona's provider can't call async snapshot. However, the render fn itself can await snapshot then call collectInjections with ctx containing the resolved kind. That keeps semantics: persona computed each turn (render each turn), and snapshot done in render.\n\nDesign: extend `InjectionContext` with:\n- `conversationId: number | string`\n- optional `chatKind?: string` (worker/chief...)\n- optional `partnerId?: string | null`\n- maybe `text?: string` for recall (user message text) — for C期 memory provider.\n\nThat's reasonable and additive.\n\nBut careful about audit endpoint: auditInjections({conversationId}) would call providers that need partnerId / chatKind / text; without them, they must degrade gracefully (return undefined) — not throw. E.g., personaProvider needs chatKind; if absent, it should return the chief persona (matching \"档案库读失败按总管处理\"). Hmm, but audit without conversation context would then show a persona row for every conversation. That's acceptable for audit? The task says the audit endpoint \"现在能列出全部注入条目\" and `source` must distinguish modules.\n\nActually a cleaner approach: separate \"static/injectable without async context\" providers vs \"context-dependent\". For audit, we can list entries that a provider produces given the minimal ctx. But entries depending on partner/worker status can't be determined without DB. Since audit is diagnostic, showing rows with lengths may be misleading.\n\nAlternative approach: build the collection in lifecycle's render/hook where all async data is available, and have the audit endpoint call a function that assembles the same list using durable... but the audit endpoint has conversationId as string (external uuid?). Actually it passes `c.req.param(\"conversationId\")` raw string, so no internal id resolution. In batch A it was a pure framework-level list.\n\nSimplest consistent approach: make providers accept a richer ctx with optional precomputed fields; the audit endpoint calls each provider with whatever's available. Providers should return undefined when their required context is missing (e.g., skills provider needs `skillsText`, which is frozen per conversation → can't compute at audit). Hmm, but then audit is \"partially empty\".\n\nHmm, maybe better: audit should reflect \"what would be injected for this conversation\". We can resolve the conversation via openChat in index? The endpoint is in index.ts and openChat exists; but this endpoint intentionally doesn't touch durable. Task 4.1 says the endpoint \"现在能列出全部注入条目\". I could resolve the internal id from external uuid using openChat (async). That would give conversationId for partner/kind. But persona/skills/topics need durable read (`input.read.snapshot(ChatKinds)`) which the section render gets from durable hook input. Outside a turn, we could use harness.snapshot(ChatKinds, internalId, CTX). Is ChatKinds a LiveDoc? Let me check shared.ts.\n\nThis is expanding a lot. Let's think about what the grading truly wants:\n1. All injections go through framework (collect → order → dedupe → channel).\n2. Byte-identical content + position.\n3. Unit tests asserting equality per site.\n4. Order snapshot test.\n5. Audit endpoint lists all entries with `source` distinguishing modules.\n6. Change record listing duplicates found.\n\nI need a design where each injection point calls the framework to obtain its text, with registrations in the registry. For section-based ones, the render calls `collectInjections([...providers], ctx).system[0]?.text` — position preserved by array index. For message-based (extra.push), render/hook calls collect on the message channel and pushes in original order.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: `collectInjections` sorts by order globally; if we call it per-injection with a single provider, order doesn't matter for output position. The task says \"顺序按 order 常量固定，并加顺序快照测试\". So order matters for the *combined* collection, e.g. when lifecycle collects all message-channel injections at once (replacing 5 extra.push calls). If we collect all 5 at once and then push in collected order, the push order may differ from original. Original order:\n1. memory (recallContext)\n2. slashManual (skill slash)\n3. recoverNote\n4. TURN_LIMIT_INSTRUCTION\n5. partner turn context\n\nBut these are mutually exclusive-ish? Not all: memory + slash + recover can coexist; turn-limit can coexist with memory; partner context requires used===1 and partnerId. If we collect all at once and sort by order, then push, the relative order must match the original sequence. So assign order constants: memory=10, slash=20, recover=30, turnLimit=40, partnerContext=50. That preserves original order. \n\nBut there's a wrinkle: original pushes happen interleaved with side effects and conditions (e.g., `used` computed between pushes; the memory/slash/recover pushes happen *before* `used` is computed; partner context requires `used===1`). Since extra array order is what matters, collecting at the end (after computing used) and pushing in order is fine as long as the *conditions* are evaluated identically. For turn-limit, the condition `MAX_TURNS>0 && used>MAX_TURNS` must still add toolsBlocked etc. We can compute entries and then push.\n\nBut also byte-identical: the content of memory depends on `lastUser` text — fine.\n\nAlso note `timestamp: Date.now()` per push — original calls Date.now() separately for each entry (could differ by ms). Tests can't assert timestamps. Keep Date.now() when pushing.\n\nNow for provider design: Since providers are sync, I'll extend InjectionContext with optional resolved facts:\n\n```ts\nexport interface InjectionContext {\n  conversationId: number | string;\n  /** 已解析的会话种类（worker / chief / …），由装配层异步取好后传入 */\n  chatKind?: string | null;\n  /** 执事 id；null = 不是执事会话 */\n  partnerId?: string | null;\n  /** 本轮最后一条用户消息文本（记忆召回/斜杠用） */\n  userText?: string;\n  /** 本轮是不是第一次模型请求（执事上下文只在 used===1 注入） */\n  firstRequest?: boolean;\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but then providers for topics need `input.read` (async). We can resolve kinds in the render and set chatKind. For `topics`, render needs to drain pushes and return text. Provider would need the derived text. Options: the provider itself calls topicsStore() — synchronous! `topicsStore().drainPendingPushes()` and `digestForInjection()` are sync. And partner checks are sync via partnersStore(). Only ChatKinds snapshot is async. So if ctx carries `chatKind`, the topics provider can be fully sync. \n\nFor `persona`: needs partnersStore (sync) + chatKind (async) + resolvePersona (sync). So ctx.chatKind suffices.\n\nFor `skills`: `skillStore?.indexFor(conversationId)` is sync (freezing is internal to store). \n\nFor `current_project`: sync.\n\nFor `verification_iron_law`: sync (partnerId + COMPANIONS_ENABLED).\n\nFor `clarify_contract`: needs partnerId + chatKind → sync with ctx.\n\nSo all 6 sections can be providers taking ctx with chatKind resolved asynchronously in the render. But careful: the original code calls `input.read.snapshot(ChatKinds, CTX)` *inside* the render (each render separately). persona does one snapshot, clarify does one, topics does one. If I hoist snapshot outside providers — each render still calls collect with its provider only, so each render does its own snapshot. To keep semantics identical (and avoid extra reads), each render computes chatKind itself then calls its provider. That's 3 snapshots as before. But persona's snapshot happens only after the partner check (early return before snapshot for non-chief partner). If I hoist the snapshot before the partner check, behavior changes (extra read, and if snapshot throws? it's awaited and would throw where previously it wouldn't). Byte-identical content preserved but semantics differ slightly. Better: keep the early-return logic inside the provider? Provider can't await.\n\nAlternative: give the provider an optional `readKind` accessor? Provider is sync so can't await.\n\nHmm. Better approach: keep the render's control flow exactly as-is, and have the render produce the text, then register it through the framework as a \"value\" — i.e., the framework also supports providers returning entries built from already-computed text. But then what's the point?\n\nLet me consider a different framing that matches the task's intent: the framework is a *catalog/registry of declaration + collection*, where each injection point obtains its entry from the framework by id. The provider can be a *function of resolved deps*. We can define providers as async-capable? `InjectionProvider` could be `(ctx) => Entry | Entry[] | undefined | Promise<...>`, and `collectInjections` async. But that would change guardProvider usage and tests... Batch A tests call collectInjections synchronously. Making it async would break them (but we can update tests). The task doesn't forbid changing the collector; but \"框架里 collectInjections 已支持 order 排序 + id 去重 + channel 分流\" — implies we should reuse as-is.\n\nHmm, but there's an inherent conflict: some injections need async data (snapshot). The clean minimal solution: extend ctx with precomputed `chatKind`, and keep each render's original control flow for *deciding* whether to call (early returns), passing the resolved value into ctx.\n\nBut that duplicates the condition logic between render and provider. Byte-identical content is still guaranteed if provider renders when called; the render decides call-vs-undefined. Duplicated conditions risk divergence but tests assert equality with pre-refactor behavior.\n\nAlternatively: I can design providers to accept a ctx with lazily precomputed optional fields AND make the render simply delegate: render awaits the data it needs, then `collectInjections([provider], ctx)`. For persona, the partner early-return is inside provider using ctx.partnerId — sync. The async snapshot is the only problem. I could resolve chatKind only when needed by always passing a \"kind resolver\" that the render awaited. Hmm.\n\nActually here's a neat trick: make the *section render* compute chatKind only if the provider might use it. For persona: original code early-returns for non-chief partner *before* snapshot. If provider handles the partner check and needs chatKind, we can still compute chatKind only after checking partner (in render) — but then render duplicates the partner check.\n\nGiven \"byte-identical content and position\" is the hard standard, and semantics preservation is called out for skills/topics/persona, I think hoisting a single `ChatKinds` snapshot per render at the top is acceptable *if* the content outcome is identical. The extra read cost: for non-chief partner sessions, persona previously did 0 snapshots; now 1. That's a behavior change in I/O only. Not byte-visible. But \"保留各自语义\" — the semantics listed are freezing/refresh/每轮现算, not snapshot counts. Still, I'd rather not add reads on hot path.\n\nBetter: use a lazy promise? The snapshot is awaited inside render, so we could pass `kind: await snapshot` only after the partner check, then provider. To avoid duplicating the partner check, put the partner check in a helper shared by render and provider? That's over-engineering.\n\nAlternative pragmatic approach: providers receive ctx with an optional async `readKind` and collectInjections stays sync — not possible.\n\nOK let me reconsider: maybe the cleanest is to allow providers to return a *thunk*? No.\n\nDecision: extend InjectionContext with optional resolved fields, and let each section's render resolve exactly the data it needs in the same order as before, then call its provider. The provider holds the *text composition + switch checks*, and the render holds only the async resolution. E.g.:\n\n```ts\n{\n  key: \"persona\",\n  render: async (input) => {\n    // 档案前置判断（同步）：非 chief 执事不注 persona\n    if (personaBlockedFor(input.conversationId)) return undefined;  // ← new helper? \n    const kinds = await input.read.snapshot(ChatKinds, CTX);\n    return collectInjections([personaProvider], { conversationId: input.conversationId, chatKind: kinds?.byInternal?.[...] }).system[0]?.text;\n  },\n}\n```\n\nHmm that still splits logic.\n\nWait. Maybe simpler and truer to \"接管\": providers are registered with `source` and take ctx; the *renders* pass a fully-resolved ctx (including async facts) to `collectInjections([provider], ctx)`. To preserve \"don't snapshot for non-chief partner\", I can make the render check partner first (as today) and only then snapshot. The provider then also checks partner — but since ctx.partnerId is already resolved, it's cheap and consistent. Slight duplication of the guard, but each guard is one line and tested.\n\nActually, we could go further: make the render compute a \"gate\" result and provider handle only content. E.g., persona render:\n\n```ts\nrender: async (input) => {\n  const ctx = await sectionContext(input);  // resolves partnerId and chatKind lazily?\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm.\n\nLet me look at how batch 26b's guard render works — it's simply `collectInjections([guardProvider], ctx).system[0]?.text`. So pattern: one provider per site, collect with single provider, take text. I'll follow that pattern, with ctx carrying resolved facts.\n\nFor audit: `auditInjections(ctx)` where ctx only has conversationId. For providers requiring chatKind/partnerId:\n- verification_iron_law: with partnerId undefined → treat as chief → VERIFICATION_IRON_LAW (same as \"档案读失败按总管处理\"). OK, reasonable for audit.\n- clarify_contract: same.\n- persona: needs chatKind; without it → chief persona. Fine (matches fallback).\n- topics: sync; partner checks need partnersStore. Without DB? It works (reads store). But drainPendingPushes has a side effect: audit would drain topic pushes and mark them pushed! That's bad — audit must be read-only. Hmm! Important: `topics` provider calling drainPendingPushes during audit would swallow pending pushes.\n\nSo the topics provider must not drain during audit. Options: add ctx flag `dryRun`/`audit`. Or: make the provider accept `drain: boolean` in ctx. For audit, set `audit: true` → digest only, no drain. Content length differs from actual (pushes omitted). Audit is diagnostic; acceptable? Hmm, \"审计端点现在能列出全部注入条目\" — entries, with lengths. Slight mismatch acceptable if documented, but better to avoid side effects.\n\nAnother option: exclude topics from audit (provider returns undefined when ctx.audit). But then audit doesn't list \"all\" entries.\n\nI think add `audit?: boolean` to ctx; topics provider when audit → uses digest only without draining (no side effect), documented. Actually `digestForInjection` — is it read-only? Let me check topics.ts.\n\nAlso skills provider in audit: indexFor may create/freeze an index (side effect: writes snapshot). Need to check skillStore.indexFor.\n\nLet me read topics.ts and skills.ts indexFor.",
