        "type": "reasoning_text",
        "text": "indexFor freezes into an in-memory map keyed by conversationId — calling it in audit would freeze the index for that conversation prematurely (before its first real turn). Side effect: if skills change between audit call and first turn, the frozen value comes from audit call. Minor but real. Also audit table uses conversationId as string param, Number(\"uuid\") = NaN; indexFor(NaN) → new entry keyed NaN. So audit shouldn't call skills provider with a bogus id... it would just create a junk frozen entry.\n\nHmm. So audit needs care.\n\nLet me step back and design the *minimal, defensible* architecture:\n\n1. `InjectionContext` gains optional resolved facts + `mode: \"live\" | \"audit\"`? Instead of a boolean `audit`, name it `dryRun`.\n\n2. Providers for static/pure things (persona text composition, verification law, clarify, current project, guard, message injections) are context-taking and pure.\n\n3. For audit: run all registered providers with a context that lacks per-conversation facts and `dryRun: true`. Providers that need conversation-specific data either compute a conservative value (chief persona, etc.) or return undefined in dryRun (skills, topics). Document in the endpoint comment: audit lists entries that don't require a live turn; context-dependent ones appear only when resolvable... Hmm, that conflicts with \"列出全部注入条目\".\n\nAlternative for audit: resolve the conversation properly. The endpoint can:\n- `openChat(externalId)` → internalId, chatKind (async). Then snapshot ChatKinds? `chatKindOf(chat)` exists. That gives \"worker\"/chief. Partner id from partnersStore. Skills indexFor(internalId) - still freezing side effect.\n\nI think `dryRun` returning undefined for skills/topics is the honest approach: audit lists what's statically determinable, and the change record documents it. But requirement 4.1 says \"能列出全部注入条目\". Hmm.\n\nWait — reconsider. Maybe audit should call providers with a ctx built from the actual conversation when available, and use dryRun only for side-effect-y providers (skills freeze is benign-ish; topics drain is not). Hmm.\n\nLet me think about what a reasonable senior engineer would do here without over-engineering:\n\n- `auditInjections(ctx)` is a *diagnostic* listing of registered injections and their sizes. It currently runs providers with `{conversationId}`. With B/C/D, providers need more ctx; audit can pass what it has and providers degrade. For skills: `indexFor(Number(id))` freezes - to avoid side effect, add `dryRun` so skills returns `renderIndex()` without freezing? That changes length? renderIndex() gives the same text as a fresh session's freeze. So audit accurately reports what a first turn would show, without freezing: good. Actually we can expose `peekIndex()`? That requires changing skills.ts (allowed? task says don't touch delegation-judge/phone-worker/delegation-api/pairing/总账; skills.ts is fine). But \"不做与本需求无关的重构\" — adding a peek is related.\n\nHmm, but simpler: in dryRun, skills provider returns undefined (documented \"按会话冻结的注入不参与审计\" - audit only lists statically computable? but skills is very much per-session).\n\nHmm, hmm. Requirement 4.1's spirit: 端点能看到\"这一轮模型看到的注入清单\". Perhaps the best: audit runs providers in dryRun and providers return the text they *would* produce; for skills we can pass a `precomputedSkills` field only available in live ctx, and in audit use a non-freezing peek. I'd rather add `peekIndex` (pure read) to skills store. That's a small, relevant change but touches the public SkillStore interface + its tests (skills.test.ts may assert shape).\n\nAlternatively let the audit endpoint accept the fact that ctx lacks `chatKind` and skills still works: `skillStore.indexFor(Number(\"uuid\"))` → NaN → freezes junk. To avoid, in dryRun we can call indexFor only if conversationId is finite numeric. In audit, conversationId is the external uuid → NaN → skip. But then audit never lists skills. Not great.\n\nHonestly, maybe the audit endpoint should resolve the chat (internal id) so it's a real per-conversation audit: `openChat` is available in index.ts (used by transcript endpoint above). With internalId and `chatKind`, we can pass `chatKind` into ctx. But `openChat` requires LLM config? No, transcript endpoint checks readLlmConfig first. openChat returns null if unknown.\n\nThen:\n- persona: partnerId + chatKind + (CHIEF or worker) → exactly what the turn would inject.\n- verify/clarify: partnerId + chatKind.\n- current_project: no ctx needed (but project is global).\n- skills: indexFor(internalId) → freezes if not yet frozen. Side effect acceptable? It's \"what will be injected\"; freezing early is the same value as the first turn's index unless skills change between the audit call and the turn. Low risk, and arguably audit of a conversation is \"warm up its index\". But a read-only endpoint shouldn't mutate. I'd add dryRun and skip skills in audit, noting it in the doc.\n\nUgh. Decisions. Let me look at what test/probe expects and keep the scope contained. The http probe will need updating anyway. I could make the audit endpoint honest: run all providers with `dryRun: true`; providers that need live-only data return undefined in dryRun (skills, topics) — and the endpoint's doc + change record state this explicitly (\"审计列的是可静态判定的注入；skills/topics 需要会话/轮次状态，审计不列（避免冻结索引 / 吞掉简报）\"). This is a defensible, documented boundary consistent with \"已知边界\" rule in AGENTS.md.\n\nBut requirement 4.1 explicitly says \"现在能列出全部注入条目\". Hmm, \"全部\" might be interpreted as \"all registered sources\" not \"every runtime entry\". With dryRun skipping two, audit would show maybe 20 of 22. That risks looking like I dodged the requirement.\n\nAlternative: audit lists an entry for skills/topics with length 0? No, dishonest.\n\nAlternative: pass a real conversation id and let audit be accurate, accepting the freeze side effect for skills, and for topics use `dryRun` to avoid draining but still render digest (content without pushes). Then topics length may differ from a turn that has pushes. Document.\n\nHmm, I keep going around. Let me look for the actual runtime test environment: http probe boots a real server and calls the endpoint with a real conversation's external id. If I make the endpoint resolve openChat, it can pass internalId and chatKind. That's more work but gives accurate persona/verification/clarify/skills.\n\nWait, but the audit endpoint's `auditInjections` runs providers — including message-channel ones (recall/slash/recover/turnlimit/partner). Those need `userText`, `used`, etc. Without a turn they're undefined → provider returns undefined. So audit will list system-channel entries only (persona, law, clarify, guard, project, skills, topics maybe) plus maybe nothing message. That's fine: message-channel injections are per-request.\n\nActually hold on. Does the task want audit to include message-channel entries? \"审计端点... 现在能列出全部注入条目\" — for a conversation at rest, the message-channel entries don't exist yet (they're per-request). Listing them with no text would be misleading. I'll list only what's computable and document. Hmm, but then \"全部\" is weak.\n\nNew idea: make audit *declarative*: `auditInjections` enumerates the registry and marks each entry with `id`, `source`, `scope`, `length`, plus `available` (whether it could be rendered without a live turn). Then entries that can't be computed now appear with length 0? Still misleading.\n\nOK. Let me settle:\n\n**Design**: `InjectionContext` = `{ conversationId, chatKind?, partnerId?, userText?, firstRequest?, turnUsed?, dryRun? }`.\n- All providers are sync and pure with respect to ctx.\n- Audit endpoint resolves the chat (internalId + chatKind) via `openChat` when possible; falls back to raw param when unknown. Passes `dryRun: true` so:\n  - topics: no drain (digest only, no pushes)\n  - skills: `indexFor` will freeze... To avoid, add `dryRun` handling: use `indexFor` only in live; in audit, return undefined? Or add `peekIndex`.\n\nLet me check whether skillStore interface change is cheap. `SkillStore` has names/manual/indexFor. Adding `peekIndex?` optional... Actually simplest: in audit, skills returns undefined with a comment. skills is a \"per-session frozen\" injection; audit can't know it without freezing. Hmm, but we could pass `freeze: false`...\n\nYou know what? The freeze is in-memory only (frozen Map inside skillStore closure). Calling indexFor(NaN) or indexFor(internalId) from audit only affects that conversation's frozen index. If audit is called on a real conversation, freezing its index at that moment is *almost always* identical to what the first turn would show, and it's a read endpoint warming a cache. But \"读端点改状态\" is a smell, and tests might not care.\n\nCompromise: add `peekIndex(conversationId)`? No — over-engineering.\n\nDecision: in `dryRun`, skills provider returns undefined and the endpoint doc says so (with reason: 冻结语义 + 读端点不改状态). Also topics in dryRun renders digest only without draining. Actually then topics length is inaccurate when pushes pending. Could return digest text only; that's the \"static\" part.\n\nHmm, wait. Is it really necessary for audit to resolve chat? If skills is skipped in dryRun anyway, the remaining providers (persona/law/clarify/project/guard/topics) need partnerId+chatKind for accuracy. Without resolution, audit shows chief persona for every conversation — wrong for partner/worker sessions. Resolving via openChat gives accuracy. openChat returns ResolvedChat with internalId; chatKindOf(chat) gives kind (async). Let me check chatKindOf signature and ResolvedChat.\n\nLet me read agent/shared.ts and session.ts openChat/chatKindOf."
      }
