text.ts` exports its providers.\n- `assist.ts` exports memory providers.\n\nBut registry must list all providers. Where? injection.ts can't import lifecycle-injections if that imports injection.ts (cycle). Put registry in a new module `agent/registry.ts` that imports all provider modules and injection.ts. Then index.ts and lifecycle.ts import the registry from registry.ts. Tests too.\n\nWait: injection.ts currently exports INJECTION_REGISTRY and index.ts imports auditInjections (which uses registry). If registry moves to registry.ts, injection.ts's auditInjections needs the registry passed in: `auditInjections(providers, ctx)`. Or keep a mutable registration API. Simpler: `auditInjections(registry, ctx)` with registry param; index.ts imports from registry.ts.\n\nHmm, but the batch A test imports INJECTION_REGISTRY from injection.ts. I'll update tests (allowed). Keep `INJECTION_REGISTRY` in a new registry module and re-export? Avoid cycles: registry.ts imports injection.ts (fine). injection.ts does not import registry.\n\nBut collectInjections needs dedupe across providers for audit. `auditInjections(registry, ctx)`.\n\nActually simpler: keep `auditInjections` in injection.ts with signature `(registry: readonly RegisteredInjection[], ctx)`.\n\n### Byte-identical strategy\n\nFor each section render, previously the text was produced by inline code. Now the provider must produce the exact same string. I'll refactor so the provider contains the original code (moved), and the render calls it via collect. Then same code path → same bytes. Tests additionally assert the provider output equals the *legacy inline computation*? The task says \"每一处接管后，内容与位置必须与改造前逐字节一致，每处都要有单测断言\". The strongest test: assert provider text equals the original source-of-truth constant/legacy function. E.g.:\n- verification_iron_law provider text === VERIFICATION_IRON_LAW.\n- persona provider === resolvePersona(...) / WORKER_PERSONA.\n- skills provider === skillStore.indexFor(id).\n- topics provider === the legacy composition (re-implemented in test? no). Better: test asserts exact expected text for a fixture (golden string), and for dynamic ones assert equals a legacy helper still exported.\n\nHmm — to prove byte-identity after refactor, the cleanest is to keep the legacy rendering function *as the provider itself* (i.e., provider calls the same pure function), and the test asserts `collect([p])[0].text === legacyFunction(...)`. But if provider IS the legacy function, test is tautological. Alternative: keep the legacy composition function in the original module (e.g. `renderTopicsSection(...)`) and make the provider a thin wrapper that calls it; the test asserts provider output === that function's output, plus a golden-text test capturing current behavior. Golden tests are the real proof of byte identity against pre-change behavior (since I can capture the strings before changing code? They're deterministic text built from constants — I can compute expected in test from the same constants, which is semi-tautological but catches ordering/separators).\n\nI think reasonable: each site test asserts the exact composed string against expectations assembled from constants + assertions on order/separators. For dynamic parts (project name), use a fixture.\n\nLet me now enumerate sites and exact code.\n\n#### B: 6 sections\n\n1. `persona` — render:\n```ts\nrender: async (input) => {\n  if (COMPANIONS_ENABLED) {\n    try {\n      const partnerId = partnersStore().partnerIdOfSession(Number(input.conversationId));\n      if (partnerId && partnerId !== CHIEF_PARTNER_ID) return undefined;\n    } catch {}\n  }\n  const kinds = await input.read.snapshot(ChatKinds, CTX);\n  if (kinds?.byInternal?.[String(input.conversationId)] === \"worker\") return WORKER_PERSONA;\n  return resolvePersona(partnersStore, PERSONA);\n}\n```\nTakeover: provider `personaProvider(ctx)`:\n```ts\n(ctx) => {\n  if (COMPANIONS_ENABLED) {\n    // ctx.partnerId resolved by caller? \n  }\n}\n```\nTo avoid the async/partner-check mess, I'll have the render resolve `partnerId` and `chatKind` exactly in the original order, then pass both into ctx and call collect:\n\n```ts\nrender: async (input) => {\n  const conversationId = Number(input.conversationId);\n  let partnerId: string | null = null;\n  if (COMPANIONS_ENABLED) {\n    try { partnerId = partnersStore().partnerIdOfSession(conversationId); } catch {}\n  }\n  if (partnerId && partnerId !== CHIEF_PARTNER_ID) return undefined;\n  const kinds = await input.read.snapshot(ChatKinds, CTX);\n  const chatKind = kinds?.byInternal?.[String(input.conversationId)] === \"worker\" ? \"worker\" : \"chief\";\n  return collectInjections([personaProvider], { conversationId, partnerId, chatKind }).system[0]?.text;\n}\n```\nWait: the original catches errors from partnerIdOfSession and treats as chief. With `let partnerId` and try/catch, same. But original returns undefined BEFORE the snapshot — preserved. And provider duplicates the partner check (harmless; ctx.partnerId already computed). Hmm, but if provider also checks `partnerId !== CHIEF` it returns undefined — consistent.\n\nHowever this duplicates the partner check in provider, and render. I'd rather have the render NOT check, and instead resolve partnerId + chatKind, provider does the check. But then snapshot happens for non-chief partners (extra I/O). Is that acceptable? It changes behavior (a read that previously didn't happen). Could throw? `snapshot` on a live session shouldn't throw. But \"保留语义\" — safest is render early-return as above. Duplication of a 2-line check is acceptable and testable.\n\nActually cleaner: provider does the full logic given ctx, and render passes a *lazy* kind? No.\n\nLet me reduce duplication by having a single shared pure decision function in the providers module:\n```ts\nexport function personaBlocked(partnerId: string | null): boolean {\n  return Boolean(partnerId && partnerId !== CHIEF_PARTNER_ID);\n}\n```\nRender uses it to early-return; provider uses it too. Same function → no divergence. Good.\n\n2. `verification_iron_law` — provider returns VERIFICATION_IRON_LAW when `lifecycleOwnsVerification(partnerId)`. Render resolves partnerId then collect.\n\n3. `clarify_contract` — needs partnerId + chatKind. Original: check switch first (before partner resolution & snapshot) → if off, return undefined *without* snapshot. Then resolve partner; if non-chief partner → undefined (no snapshot). Then snapshot; worker → undefined; else clarifyContractFor(partnerId). Preserve: render resolves partner first (as original: clarify switch check → partner → snapshot), computes chatKind, then collect. Provider: checks enabled, partner block, worker block, then returns clarifyContractFor.\n\nCareful: `clarifyContractFor(partnerId)` returns CLARIFY_CONTRACT or undefined for non-chief? Let's check delegation.ts clarifyContractFor: it returns undefined for partnerId non-chief, but provider already handled. Fine.\n\n4. `current_project` — pure sync. Provider builds text; render = `collectInjections([currentProjectProvider], ctx).system[0]?.text`. Exact same string composition.\n\n5. `skills` — provider: `skillStore?.indexFor(Number(ctx.conversationId))`. In dryRun return undefined to avoid freezing. Render passes frozen semantics naturally (indexFor freezes internally). \n\n6. `topics` — provider needs partner/group checks, chatKind, store drain + digest + lines. Move the whole body into a pure-ish function `renderTopicsSection(conversationId, opts)` in... topics.ts? lifecycle-injections? The body uses wrapUntrustedObjective, TOPICS_ENABLED, topicsStore, partnersStore. I'll put the provider in lifecycle-injections.ts, calling a `renderTopicsSection` function that does the original logic given `{conversationId, partnerId, group, chatKind, drain}`. Render resolves group/partner/chatKind? Original order: TOPICS_ENABLED → group check → partner check → snapshot. So render: if !TOPICS_ENABLED → undefined (provider also checks); then resolve group/partner (sync, try/catch); if group or non-chief partner → undefined; then snapshot chatKind; then collect. Provider re-checks with ctx.\n\nHmm, render duplicating checks. Alternatively: since partnersStore calls are sync and cheap, and the only async is snapshot, I can do:\n```ts\nrender: async (input) => {\n  const ctx = await resolveSectionContext(input); // resolves partnerId, group, chatKind\n  return collectInjections([topicsProvider], ctx).system[0]?.text;\n}\n```\nwhere `resolveSectionContext` always snapshots. For topics, original snapshots only after group/partner checks. Snapshot cost for partner/group sessions: minor. But `used`? no.\n\nI'll accept resolve-then-collect for topics and clarify (both early-return before snapshot only for non-chief/group/worker), because those sessions are less frequent and snapshot is a cheap durable read (already cached in-memory LiveDoc?). Hmm, `input.read.snapshot` reads the session doc — cheap. But \"behavior changes\" purists...\n\nActually, safest for *content* and also for read-behavior: keep early returns in render (using shared predicates), then snapshot. I'll write small shared predicate helpers in lifecycle-injections.ts used by both render and provider. That's clean enough: one predicate, one provider, no duplicated logic.\n\nWait, but if provider checks `ctx.partnerId` etc., and render already returned for blocked cases, provider's check is just defense. Fine.\n\nLet me now decide where providers live:\n\n**`agent/sections.ts`** (new): all 6 section providers + `SECTION_IDS` + predicates. Imports: assist (PERSONA/WORKER_PERSONA/skillStore), partners, projects/shared currentProject, topics, turn-gate, injection-guard? No guard (stays in injection.ts).\n\nHmm, injection.ts already has guardProvider. I'll keep guard there and put the rest in `agent/lifecycle-injections.ts`. Name: `sections.ts` might be vague; `lifecycle-injections.ts` is explicit.\n\n**`agent/assist.ts`**: export `memoryProvider` (recallContext) and `taskMemoryProvider` (taskMemoryBlock). The former needs userText + conversationId. But recallContext takes internal numeric id. In lifecycle ctx.conversationId is number. In audit ctx.conversationId may be string uuid → Number() = NaN → recallContext(NaN, \"\") returns \"\" because no text. OK.\n\ntaskMemoryBlock: used in openai-compat, not in a \"turn\". Should it be in the registry/providers? It is an injection (task instructions tail). Takeover: openai-compat builds `collectInjections([taskMemoryProvider], {conversationId: \"task\"})...`? That's silly — collector adds nothing. But requirement says take it over: entry with id `task_memory`, channel \"system\"? It's appended to instructions, not messages. I can have the provider return text, and openai-compat uses `collectInjections([taskMemoryProvider], {conversationId: 0}).system[0]?.text` instead of calling taskMemoryBlock directly. That's a mechanical takeover preserving bytes:\n```ts\nconst memory = task.memory === \"diary\" ? (collectInjections([taskMemoryProvider], {conversationId: 0}).system[0]?.text ?? \"\") : \"\";\n```\nProvider returns undefined when text is \"\". Same as before (`taskMemoryBlock()` returns \"\" → memory falsy). Good, byte-identical.\n\nBut is that \"assist 的记忆召回注入（3 处）\"? I'll frame the change record inventory honestly: assist.ts 提供 2 个注入口，实际接管 2 处（recallContext → lifecycle beforeRequest；taskMemoryBlock → openai-compat 任务通道）。Plus maybe the third: `PERSONA`/`WORKER_PERSONA` are consumed by persona section (B期). So \"assist 涉及 3 处\" could be persona 常量 + recall + taskMemory. That reconciles the count! I'll note that.\n\n**`agent/partner-context.ts`**: providers for soul? No, soul goes to instructions (not an injection into messages). Segments injected via text: contract, goal, discipline(8 blocks), status, guard. Takeover: `buildPartnerTurnContext` assembles from `collectInjections(PARTNER_CONTEXT_PROVIDERS, ctx).message` joined by \"\\n\\n\". Each provider returns one segment with order constants; entries with undefined text omitted. Dedupe within this collection: e.g., if two providers emit same id → merged. Are there internal duplicates? verification iron law appears only in discipline (chief skips). Guard provider id `injection_guard` — same id as the section guard. Within this collection (message channel) only one guard → fine.\n\nBut careful: `collectInjections` joins? No, it returns array; we join with \"\\n\\n\". Original joined [contract, goal, discipline, status, guard] with \"\\n\\n\", where discipline itself is blocks joined by \"\\n\\n\". If I flatten discipline into individual providers, joining all with \"\\n\\n\" yields exactly the same string (since discipline = blocks.join(\"\\n\\n\") and text = parts.join(\"\\n\\n\")). Yes: [a, [b,c,d].join(\"\\n\\n\"), e].join(\"\\n\\n\") === [a,b,c,d,e].join(\"\\n\\n\"). Byte-identical. \n\nOrder constants: contract=100, goal=110, verification=120, ruling=130, workSilence=140, grounding=150, artifact=160, ownerData=170, briefCard=180, attentionOneQuestion=190, status=200, guard=210. Matches original order.\n\nThe `buildPartnerTurnContext` return fields (contract/goal/discipline/status/guard) must stay for tests. So assembly: collect all, then derive fields from ids. Hmm, discipline field = join of discipline ids. That's fine:\n\n```ts\nconst collected = collectInjections(PARTNER_CONTEXT_PROVIDERS, ctx).message;\nconst byId = new Map(...);\nconst discipline = DISCIPLINE_IDS.map(id=>byId.get(id)?.text).filter(Boolean).join(\"\\n\\n\");\nconst contract = byId.get(\"partner_contract\")?.text ?? null; ...\nconst text = collected.map(e=>e.text).join(\"\\n\\n\") || undefined;\n```\nCareful: original `text` is undefined if all empty; but discipline is always non-empty (ATTENTION_ONE_QUESTION always present, unless blocked... blocks: verification may be undefined for chief, RULING always, WORK_SILENCE always, GROUNDING always, ARTIFACT maybe off, OWNER maybe off, BRIEF maybe off, ATTENTION always). So text always non-empty. Fine.\n\nAlso `guard`: `injectionGuardEnabled() ? INJECTION_GUARD : null`. Provider returns entry with id \"injection_guard\". But if this collection ever merges with lifecycle's system collection, dedupe would nuke one. We won't merge across channels in one call, so OK. But the *audit* registry will have two sources with entry id \"injection_guard\" (lifecycle guard + partner guard). Dedupe via `auditInjections` running each provider separately (current implementation) keeps both rows but with same id and different source. That's a duplicate to report.\n\nHmm, the task explicitly wants duplicates found. The chief-guard duplication: for chief linked session, both lifecycle section `injection_guard` (system) and partner-context guard (message) are injected. Is that actually true? Let's verify `partnerIdOfSession` for the chief's main conversation. ensureChiefPartner links? Let me check partners.ts ensureChiefPartner and how the main conversation maps to chief.",
