        "text": "Good: tests can set PARTNERS_DB_PATH + PARTNER_HOMES_ROOT.\n\nNow skills provider: `skillStore` is module-level in assist.ts, created from real dirs on import. In test env it may load real skills dir (read-only) - existing tests import assist? prompt-discipline imports partner-context which imports... not assist. injection test importing lifecycle-injections imports assist → creates skillStore and memoryEngine (opens .data/memory?). Hmm, that's bad: `memoryEngine` init at module import with MEMORY_DB_PATH default `.data/memory.sqlite` — would create real files in tests. Existing tests avoid importing assist. But lifecycle-injections imports assist (PERSONA/skillStore). And injection.test imports lifecycle-injections → side effects.\n\nTo avoid, set env in the test before dynamic import: `MEMORY_ENABLED=false`, `AGENT_SKILLS_DIR=<tmp>`, `CHIEF_PARTNER=false`? skillStore creation logs and reads dirs; setting AGENT_SKILLS_DIR to temp keeps it isolated. Let me check resolveSkillsDir uses AGENT_SKILLS_DIR and user dirs (may create them!). \"首访自动建\" — could create %USERPROFILE%/.agents/skills? That might be a side effect on the dev machine. Existing tests that import tools.ts pass a stub SkillStore, avoiding assist.\n\nAlternative: avoid importing assist in lifecycle-injections by passing persona text in ctx? E.g., providers need PERSONA/WORKER_PERSONA constants which live in assist.ts. I could move those constants to `agent/persona-text.ts` and re-export from assist (barrel), so lifecycle-injections imports the text module without triggering memory/skills singletons. But skillStore provider needs skillStore — that's inherently in assist.\n\nHmm. For skills, the provider could be defined *in assist.ts* (`skillsProvider`) and imported by lifecycle-injections → still imports assist. No escape: lifecycle must import skillStore anyway (it already does: `skillStore` imported from assist in lifecycle.ts). So lifecycle.ts already imports assist, meaning the server module graph already pulls assist. Only tests are the concern.\n\nFor the injection test, I'll set env before dynamic import:\n```ts\nprocess.env.MEMORY_ENABLED = \"false\";\nprocess.env.AGENT_SKILLS_DIR = join(tmp, \"skills\");\nprocess.env.PARTNERS_DB_PATH = ...\nprocess.env.PARTNER_HOMES_ROOT = ...\nprocess.env.PARTNERS_DB_PATH ...\n```\nThat keeps it isolated. skillStore then has no skills (empty temp dir) → indexFor returns undefined. Good for testing \"no skills → undefined\".\n\nBut the persona test: `resolvePersona` with no chief record → returns PERSONA fallback. Good: assert provider text === PERSONA. And with `chatKind: \"worker\"` → WORKER_PERSONA.\n\ntopics: topicsStore() opens .data/topics.json? Set TOPICS_DB_PATH? Let me check topicsStore env. Could be a JSON file. Let me check."
      }
    ],
