      "reasoning": "`chatKindOf` returns \"partner\" for partner sessions (not \"chief\"/\"worker\"). So my chatKind type should be \"chief\" | \"worker\" | \"partner\". For persona section, original logic: partnerId non-chief → undefined; else if kinds worker → WORKER_PERSONA; else persona. A chief-linked session: chatKind \"partner\" but partnerId chief → persona. So use partnerId primarily, not chatKind \"partner\".\n\nSimplify ctx:\n```ts\nexport interface InjectionContext {\n  conversationId: number | string;\n  /** 会话种类表里的值（worker / chief）；缺省 chief。 */\n  chatKind?: \"chief\" | \"worker\" | null;\n  partnerId?: string | null;   // 执事 id（含 chief）；null/undefined = 无链接\n  group?: boolean;             // 群会话\n  userText?: string;\n  turn?: number;\n  dryRun?: boolean;\n}\n```\nIn index.ts audit, resolve: internalId, kinds snapshot via chatKindOf? That returns partner. Better: directly read ChatKinds? `harness.snapshot(ChatKinds, CTX)` requires harness access from index. Simpler: `chatKindOf(chat)` → if \"worker\" set worker else chief. And partnerId from partnersStore.\n\nHmm, is this too much for the audit endpoint? Let me consider not resolving and just documenting that audit uses the raw conversation id (as batch A did). Then partner providers return undefined (no partnerId), persona shows chief fallback, skills skipped in dryRun, topics would show chief topics... For a partner session, audit would be wrong. I think resolving is worth ~10 lines.\n\nBut `openChat` calls getHarness → opens Harness (needs LLM config; returns null otherwise). If null, fall back to raw. Fine. Note: getHarness opening on a read endpoint that previously was dependency-free is a behavior change (heavy). Hmm. The endpoint is diagnostic; acceptable? It could open the DB on first call. I'd rather avoid surprising heavy work but accurate audit requires it.\n\nAlternative: keep audit dependency-free, and have the *test/probe* assert only framework-level entries (guard, persona fallback, current_project, verification law, clarify) that don't need conversation state. Document \"按会话冻结 / 需要轮次状态的注入（skills / topics / 5 处消息注入 / 执事在办）在审计里省略\". That's honest and minimal. But requirement 4.1 \"列出全部注入条目\"...\n\nUgh. I'll go with resolving the chat — it makes the endpoint genuinely useful and matches \"全部\". But then skills: in dryRun skip (documented). topics: dryRun digest only (no drain). Those two are \"conditional by design\".\n\nHmm wait, if I resolve the chat, why not let skills render? Because freezing. I could call `indexFor` — the freeze is idempotent and in-memory; the frozen value for the conversation would be set at audit time. If skills change between audit and first turn, the frozen index is stale — but audit is a deliberate diagnostic action, so the user is effectively saying \"show me this conversation's injections\". I'll still skip in dryRun to keep the read endpoint side-effect free. Document.\n\nOK. Now let me also handle: `auditInjections` runs each provider in its own collection (current code) → duplicates across sources appear twice. With dedupe per source, the guard appears twice (lifecycle source and partner-context source). That's exactly how we surface the duplicate in audit. After merging (removing partner guard), only one remains. Good.\n\nNow let me think about `persona` provider and `resolvePersona(partnersStore, PERSONA)` — requires partnersStore; called per turn (already the case).\n\n### Order snapshot test\n\nTest file `agent/injection-order.test.ts`? Simpler: add to injection.test.ts:\n```ts\nconst EXPECTED = [\"injection_guard\",\"persona\",...] sorted by order\n```\nAssert `INJECTION_REGISTRY` entries' ids in sorted order equal a frozen list. Note: registry entries are providers, not entries; need to call each provider with a ctx where all return something. Some return undefined (skills/topics/partner). Hmm. Instead snapshot `INJECTION_ORDER` keys → values sorted. That's a true snapshot of the order asset.\n\nI'll define:\n```ts\nexport const INJECTION_ORDER = { ... } as const;\n```\nand test `Object.entries(INJECTION_ORDER).sort((a,b)=>a[1]-b[1]).map(([k])=>k)` equals frozen array. Plus assert no duplicate order values.\n\nGood enough (\"照 tool-schema-guard 的做法\" — freeze the ordered list).\n\n### Files and tests for byte identity\n\nTest cases (in injection.test.ts, extended):\n1. `guardProvider.text === INJECTION_GUARD` (existing).\n2. `personaProvider({chatKind:\"chief\", partnerId:null}).text === PERSONA` (fallback when no chief record? resolvePersona may find a chief if .data exists. To be deterministic, set PARTNER paths to temp so no chief → fallback PERSONA). For worker → WORKER_PERSONA.\n3. `verificationProvider({partnerId:null}).text === VERIFICATION_IRON_LAW`; non-chief partner → undefined.\n4. `clarifyProvider({partnerId:null, chatKind:\"chief\"}).text === CLARIFY_CONTRACT`; worker → undefined; off switch → undefined.\n5. `projectProvider` text equals the exact 3-line join / 杂事模式 string.\n6. `skillsProvider` in dryRun → undefined; live with skills store stub? skillStore is module-level real (temp dir empty) → undefined. Can't easily test non-empty without touching skills.ts. I could test that `skillsProvider` returns `skillStore?.indexFor(...)` by comparing to skillStore.indexFor directly — with empty store both undefined. Weak but byte-equal by construction. Hmm, for a stronger test, I can create a temp skills dir with a valid skill file before importing? The skill file format requires a summary line. Let me check skills.ts scan format.\n\nMaybe simpler: test `skillsProvider` returns same as `skillStore?.indexFor(id)` for the same id (identity), plus golden test for a temp skill (if feasible).\n\n7. `topicsProvider` text equals the legacy composition for a temp topics store with no active topics → \"在跟的课题：（暂无）\\n\\n课题嗅探（…）…\". And with a pending push, assert drain happens (semantics!) and text structure.\n\n8. Message providers:\n   - `memoryRecallProvider` with MEMORY_ENABLED=false → recallContext \"\" → undefined.\n   - `skillSlashProvider({userText:\"/foo rest\", ...})` → exact template using skillStore.manual(\"foo\") → undefined unless skill exists. With empty store, undefined. Hmm.\n   - `recoverNoteProvider` — takeRecoverNote: test markRecoverResume then provider returns exact note; second call undefined (once semantics).\n   - `turnLimitProvider({turn: MAX_TURNS+1})` → TURN_LIMIT_INSTRUCTION; turn 1 → undefined.\n   - `partnerContextProvider` — needs DB; use buildPartnerTurnContext with deps and compare text.\n\n9. Partner-context segments: assert `collectInjections(PARTNER_CONTEXT_PROVIDERS, ctx).message.map(text).join(\"\\n\\n\") === buildPartnerTurnContext(...).text` (tautological if build uses it) — instead assert individual segment texts equal constants, and order of joined text matches original ordering: contract < goal < law < ruling < ... < status < guard. Plus golden assembly: construct expected string manually from constants and compare to `text`. That's strong.\n\nLet me write `expected = [contractText, goalText, VERIFICATION_IRON_LAW, RULING, WORK_SILENCE, GROUNDING, ARTIFACT, OWNER, (brief if non-chief), ATTENTION, statusText].join(\"\\n\\n\")` and compare with context.text. statusText is dynamic but available via `context.status`. So expected uses context.status → semi. But content equality of other segments is the point.\n\n10. Position: section array order test. Export `LIFECYCLE_SECTION_KEYS` and assert lifecycle's extension sections order. But the extension is built inside openHarness (requires LLM config + DB). Can't easily inspect. Instead I can extract the section definitions into a constant array (still inside openHarness closure because they use `input`). Move the sections array to a function `buildSections()`? It doesn't close over anything except imports. I can define `export const LIFECYCLE_SECTIONS = [...]` at module scope in lifecycle.ts (uses only module imports) and reference it in defineExtension. Then test imports it and asserts keys order + that each render equals the provider text for a fake input (fake `input.read.snapshot` + conversationId). That's a strong byte-identity + position test! Yes — do that.\n\nBut `LIFECYCLE_SECTIONS` typed as expected by defineExtension (`SectionDefinition[]`?). Type inference may need care. I'll type it loosely with the same shape (key, render). Let me check the Extension section type from pi-durable. Just use inline array hoisted to module scope with `as const`? defineExtension expects an array; inference fine if I type via `Parameters<typeof defineExtension>[0][\"sections\"]`? Might be complex. Let me inspect the type quickly.",
