        "text": "Now let's write. First inject the context/order changes into injection.ts.\n\nI'll rewrite injection.ts wholesale carefully, preserving existing behavior/comments.\n\nThen lifecycle-injections.ts.\n\nProvider for `topics` — text composition includes `wrapUntrustedObjective`; needs `TOPICS_ENABLED`, `topicsStore`. Note `TOPICS_ENABLED` is computed at import (env at import). Fine.\n\nFor audit dryRun in topics: use digest only, no drain:\n```ts\nconst pushes = ctx.dryRun ? [] : store.drainPendingPushes();\n```\nDocument.\n\nNow `current_project`: uses `currentProjectEnabled` and `currentProject()` from shared.ts. Provider returns exact strings.\n\n`skills`: `skillStore?.indexFor(Number(ctx.conversationId))`; dryRun → undefined.\n\n`persona`: \n```ts\nexport const personaProvider: InjectionProvider = (ctx) => {\n  const partnerId = ctx.partnerId ?? null;\n  if (partnerId && partnerId !== CHIEF_PARTNER_ID) return undefined;\n  if (ctx.chatKind === \"worker\") return entry(WORKER_PERSONA);\n  return resolvePersona(partnersStore, PERSONA);\n};\n```\nOriginal: partner check uses COMPANIONS_ENABLED; ctx.partnerId computed by caller using COMPANIONS_ENABLED. In audit/index resolution also gated. But direct unit tests may pass partnerId without COMPANIONS_ENABLED — fine.\n\nHmm, subtle: original `if (COMPANIONS_ENABLED) { try { partnerId = ... } catch {} }`. If COMPANIONS_ENABLED false → partnerId null → chief persona. Our helper `partnerIdOf(conversationId)` returns null when disabled. Good.\n\n`verification`: `lifecycleOwnsVerification(ctx.partnerId ?? null) ? VERIFICATION_IRON_LAW : undefined`.\n\n`clarify`: \n```ts\nif (!clarifyContractEnabled()) return undefined;\nconst partnerId = ctx.partnerId ?? null;\nif (partnerId && partnerId !== CHIEF_PARTNER_ID) return undefined;\nif (ctx.chatKind === \"worker\") return undefined;\nreturn clarifyContractFor(partnerId) ?? undefined;  // returns string|undefined\n```\nReturn entry only if text.\n\n`injection_guard` guardProvider stays in injection.ts.\n\nNow message providers in lifecycle-injections.ts or a separate `message-injections.ts`? Group them in the same file `lifecycle-injections.ts` since they're all lifecycle's. It imports takeRecoverNote, TURN_LIMIT_INSTRUCTION/MAX_TURNS, skillSlashOf, recallContext, buildPartnerTurnContext, workSummaryFor, COMPANIONS_ENABLED, partnersStore. Wait, partner context provider calls buildPartnerTurnContext (partner-context.ts) which imports injection.ts → cycle? lifecycle-injections → partner-context → injection; lifecycle-injections → injection. No cycle (partner-context doesn't import lifecycle-injections).\n\nBut partner-context imports injection.ts; and injection-registry imports lifecycle-injections and partner-context. Fine.\n\n`memoryRecallProvider`: uses ctx.userText. In lifecycle render/hook we set userText. \n\n`recoverNoteProvider`: `takeRecoverNote(Number(ctx.conversationId))`; if dryRun → undefined.\n\n`turnLimitProvider`: `const turn = ctx.turn ?? 0; if (MAX_TURNS > 0 && turn > MAX_TURNS) return entry(TURN_LIMIT_INSTRUCTION);`\n\n`partnerContextProvider`: \n```ts\nif (ctx.dryRun) return undefined;\nif (ctx.turn !== 1 || !COMPANIONS_ENABLED) return undefined;\nconst partnerId = ctx.partnerId ?? null;  // caller may resolve\nif (!partnerId) return undefined;\ntry { const turnContext = buildPartnerTurnContext(partnerId, Number(ctx.conversationId), { workSummary: workSummaryFor }); if (!turnContext.text) return undefined; return entry(turnContext.text); }\ncatch { console.warn(same message); return undefined; }\n```\nBut the original try/catch wraps `partnersStore().partnerIdOfSession` too, with the same warning message. If we resolve partnerId outside the try in lifecycle, errors would throw. So lifecycle should compute partnerId with try/catch and pass it; if the store throws, partnerId = null (log? original logs \"上下文装配失败\"). Hmm, original logs once for any failure. I'll keep the whole block in the provider:\n\n```ts\nexport const partnerContextProvider: InjectionProvider = (ctx) => {\n  if (ctx.dryRun || ctx.turn !== 1 || !COMPANIONS_ENABLED) return undefined;\n  const conversationId = Number(ctx.conversationId);\n  try {\n    const partnerId = ctx.partnerId !== undefined ? ctx.partnerId : partnersStore().partnerIdOfSession(conversationId);\n    if (!partnerId) return undefined;\n    const turnContext = buildPartnerTurnContext(partnerId, conversationId, { workSummary: workSummaryFor });\n    if (!turnContext.text) return undefined;\n    return entry(turnContext.text, ...);\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.warn(`[partner] 会话 ${conversationId} 上下文装配失败: ...`);\n    return undefined;\n  }\n};\n```\nMessage identical to original. \n\nNote: original passes `api.conversationId` (number). Good.\n\nSimilarly `memoryRecallProvider` must not be gated by COMPANIONS; recallContext handles it.\n\n`skillSlashProvider`:\n```ts\n(ctx) => {\n  const name = skillSlashOf(ctx.userText ?? \"\");\n  const manual = name ? skillStore?.manual(name) : undefined;\n  if (!name || !manual) return undefined;\n  return entry(`【技能调用 · ${name}】\\n${manual}\\n（用户消息开头的 /${name} 就是调用这份手册；其余文字是这一轮的指令。）`);\n}\n```\nEXACT original template.\n\n`memoryRecallProvider`:\n```ts\n(ctx) => {\n  const text = recallContext(Number(ctx.conversationId), ctx.userText ?? \"\");\n  return text ? entry(text) : undefined;\n}\n```\nOriginal used `lastUser ? contentText(...) : \"\"` — same.\n\nImportant: the original computes `lastUser` once; provider recomputes from ctx.userText. Fine.\n\nNow, in lifecycle beforeRequest, we need the collected list to be in original order. `collectInjections(MESSAGE_PROVIDERS, ctx)` with orders memory=100, slash=110, recover=120, turnLimit=130, partnerContext=140. Dedupe: ids unique.\n\nBut wait: `recoverNoteProvider` consumes the note inside collect — even if `extra` ends up... fine.\n\nAlso side effect ordering: original `takeRecoverNote` runs before `beginRelayRun`/`used`. Now it runs after those (collect at end). `takeRecoverNote` is independent. OK.\n\nBut `used > MAX_TURNS` also adds toolsBlocked/limitHits/aborting — keep that logic before collect, and only the *entry* comes from provider.\n\nNow the audit: message providers need turn/userText; audit ctx lacks → all undefined except turnLimit (turn undefined → 0 → no). Good.\n\n### partner-context rewrite\n\nProviders need deps (store, approvals, workRoot, workSummary) and conversationId/partnerId from ctx. Define a factory:\n\n```ts\nexport function partnerTurnProviders(deps: PartnerContextDeps = {}): RegisteredInjection[] { ... }\n```\nBut `RegisteredInjection` has source; for collection we only need providers. Use `InjectionProvider[]`.\n\nEach provider:\n- contract: `renderContractSection(current?.contractSource ?? store.readContractFromHome(partnerId))`.\n  But `current` (version) is read once in buildPartnerTurnContext. If providers each read the store, repeated reads (cheap SQLite but inconsistent snapshot). To preserve exactly, `buildPartnerTurnContext` should read `current` once and close over it. So providers must be created per-call with resolved values:\n\n```ts\nfunction providersFor(partnerId, deps, current): InjectionProvider[]\n```\nBut then registry audit can't reuse them without a call. For audit, `auditPartnerTurn(partnerId)` could call `buildPartnerTurnContext`? That returns text only, not entries.\n\nSimplify: keep `buildPartnerTurnContext` reading `current` then creating providers with the resolved values:\n\n```ts\nexport function buildPartnerTurnContext(partnerId, conversationId, deps = {}) {\n  const store = deps.store ?? partnersStore();\n  const current = store.currentVersion(partnerId);\n  const soul = ...;\n  const contractText = renderContractSection(current?.contractSource ?? store.readContractFromHome(partnerId));\n  const goalText = store.get(partnerId)?.currentGoal?.trim() ? `【当前目标】${...}` : undefined;\n  const disciplineTexts = [verificationIronLawFor, RULING, WORK_SILENCE, GROUNDING, artifact?, owner?, brief?, ATTENTION]\n  const statusText = buildPartnerStatusSection(...);\n  const guardText = injectionGuardEnabled() ? INJECTION_GUARD : null;  // 合并后移除\n  const entries = collectInjections(makeProviders({...}), ctx).message;\n  ...\n}\n```\nThis is essentially the same code, just routed through providers. The providers take precomputed values from ctx? Adding all those to InjectionContext is ugly. Instead, provider factory closes over values:\n\n```ts\nconst providers: InjectionProvider[] = [\n  () => entry(PARTNER_CONTRACT_ID, contractText, order),\n  ...\n];\n```\nThat's a thin wrapper = framework is just sorting. It satisfies \"接管\" mechanically (entries flow through collect), with order/dedupe applied. It's honest: the framework now owns ordering + dedupe; content generation stays where it was.\n\nHmm, is that \"接管\"? The entry text is produced by the same functions. Yes: each segment becomes an InjectionEntry with id/order/scope/channel, collected+sorted+deduped by the framework. Good enough and low risk.\n\nFor audit: add `export function partnerInjectionProviders(partnerId, deps?)` that computes the same values? That duplicates. Alternatively audit just skips partner-context (since without a live partner ctx). Hmm.\n\nLet me define the factory public:\n\n```ts\nexport interface PartnerInjectionValues { contract?, goal?, discipline: Array<[id,text]>, status?, }\nexport function partnerContextProviders(values): InjectionProvider[]\n```\nNot great.\n\nAlternative: buildPartnerTurnContext keeps its field computation, then:\n```ts\nconst entries = collectInjections(partnerContextProviders({\n  contract: contract ?? undefined,\n  goal: goal ?? undefined,\n  disciplineBlocks: [...],\n  status: status ?? undefined,\n}), ctx).message;\n```\nAnd for audit, I'll call a helper `auditPartnerContext(partnerId, conversationId)`? Hmm, registry needs a provider with only InjectionContext. We can give partner providers access to `ctx.partnerId` and for audit resolve from store — but values need `current` etc. \n\nDecision: for audit, partner-context providers are registered as a **single** provider `partner_context` that requires `ctx.partnerId`, `ctx.dryRun !== true`? No...\n\nLet me simplify the whole audit story: **audit lists entries from providers that can render with the given ctx**. Partner providers need `ctx.partnerId`; I'll implement them to compute from store when ctx.partnerId is present (reading store each time). For buildPartnerTurnContext, the same providers are used with the same ctx → they'd read the store per provider (5-12 reads). Each `store.currentVersion` is a SQLite query; 12 queries per turn is wasteful. Hmm, but partnersStore is local SQLite; still, avoid.\n\nOK compromise: partner providers are created by a factory that accepts an optional `deps`+precomputed snapshot. For audit, a separate exported function `partnerAuditProviders()` constructs providers that read defaults. But registry entries must be static: `{ source: \"partner-context\", provider: (ctx) => ... }` where the provider builds values from ctx (store reads). Then `buildPartnerTurnContext` could pass a memoized ctx? No.\n\nLet me measure the real cost: buildPartnerTurnContext is called once per turn (used===1). If a provider re-reads `currentVersion` 4 times, that's 4 SQLite point queries per turn — acceptable? It's a local DB, ~microseconds. But \"不做与本需求无关的重构\" and hidden perf changes... \n\nAlternative cleaner architecture: providers receive the values via a **module-level per-call map keyed by conversationId**? Ugly.\n\nBetter: make `InjectionProvider` accept an optional second argument `input` (precomputed values):\n\n```ts\nexport type InjectionProvider = (ctx: InjectionContext, input?: InjectionInput) => ...\n```\nNo—collectInjections calls provider(ctx) only.\n\nOK: define partner providers with a *closure factory*:\n\n```ts\nexport function partnerContextProviders(partnerId: string, deps: PartnerContextDeps = {}, current?: PartnerVersion | null): InjectionProvider[]\n```\nand registry cannot use it directly. For audit, register a **summary provider** `partner_context` that returns `undefined` unless ctx.partnerId, then computes via `partnerContextProviders(partnerId, {}, store.currentVersion(...))` and collects internally to build one entry? But then audit shows one row instead of 11.\n\nRequirement 4.2 only says source distinguishes modules, not that each segment is a row. So audit can show one `partner_context` row per conversation with total length. That's acceptable and simpler: a registered provider `partnerContextAuditProvider(ctx)` that, when `ctx.partnerId` exists, builds the full turn context text (dryRun: status undefined?) and returns one entry with id \"partner_context\". But lengths differ from live (status omitted in dryRun). Hmm.\n\nHonestly, maybe audit shouldn't include partner-context at all, and requirement 4.2's \"source 字段能区分来源模块\" is satisfied by the sources that do appear (lifecycle/assist). But they explicitly list partner-context/settled as sources to distinguish.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe audit should be a **catalog** (what injections *exist*), not per-conversation values. Then rows don't need text lengths... but the shape includes length.\n\nOK, final decision, pragmatic:\n- `auditInjections` uses providers with a ctx carrying resolved conversation facts. For partner-context, register `partnerContextProvider` that (a) requires ctx.partnerId, (b) returns ONE entry `id: \"partner_context\"`, `text: turnContext.text` built with `buildPartnerTurnContext` — no dryRun suppression for content, but pass `dryRun` to skip status? No: in audit, approvals/workRoot scanning is heavy but bounded (limit 200). Hmm, the endpoint is admin-only-ish. I'll skip status in dryRun? That changes length.\n\nActually you know what: audit for a real conversation is rare, and scanning runs dirs is exactly what the turn does. Let it compute the full thing (no dryRun effect in partner context). It gives the most accurate audit. Only topics has the drain side effect (must avoid) and skills has freeze (avoid). So dryRun only affects those two. Cleaner semantics: \"dryRun = 不做有副作用的渲染（课题 drain、技能冻结）\".\n\nThen partner-context audit shows one row with total length, id \"partner_context\", source \"partner-context\". Good enough; documented.\n\nBut then within `buildPartnerTurnContext` I still want 11 entries for ordering/dedupe. So:\n- Live assembly uses per-segment providers (closure factory).\n- Audit uses one aggregate provider.\n\nThat means the registry's partner source doesn't reflect segments. Acceptable? The audit's purpose is \"这一轮模型看到了什么\" — one aggregate row with total length + id is less informative but honest. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: audit the segments too by using the factory inside the aggregate provider, but `collectInjections` in audit loops per provider and can't return 11 from an aggregate? It can: provider may return `InjectionEntry[]`. So `partnerContextAuditProvider(ctx)` can return the 11 entries from the factory! Then audit lists all 11 (with ids/orders). And live assembly uses the same factory. That's the clean solution:\n\n```ts\n// partner-context.ts\nexport function partnerContextEntries(partnerId, conversationId, deps, current): InjectionEntry[] {\n  // build segments, return entries\n}\nexport function buildPartnerTurnContext(...) {\n  const entries = partnerContextEntries(...);\n  // derive contract/goal/discipline/status/guard from entries by id\n}\nexport const partnerContextProvider: InjectionProvider = (ctx) => {\n  if (!ctx.partnerId) return undefined;\n  const deps = {};\n  const store = partnersStore();\n  const current = store.currentVersion(ctx.partnerId);\n  return partnerContextEntries(ctx.partnerId, Number(ctx.conversationId), {...deps, store}, current);\n};\n```\nAnd registry uses `partnerContextProvider`. In lifecycle, `buildPartnerTurnContext` is called (which internally uses entries). Audit uses the provider. Slight duplication of the \"store/current\" setup but bounded, and `buildPartnerTurnContext` could also call `partnerContextProvider` with a ctx.partnerId! Then:\n\n```ts\nexport function buildPartnerTurnContext(partnerId, conversationId, deps = {}) {\n  const ctx = { conversationId, partnerId };\n  const entries = collectInjections(partnerContextProviders(deps), ctx).message;\n  ...\n}\n```\nwhere `partnerContextProviders(deps)` closes over deps and each provider handles one segment, reading the store lazily. Reading store per provider = multiple reads. To avoid, `partnerContextProviders` can memoize the store reads in a closure (lazy once):\n\n```ts\nfunction partnerContextProviders(deps) {\n  const store = deps.store ?? partnersStore();\n  let cached: PartnerVersion | null | undefined;  // lazy\n  const current = () => (cached === undefined ? (cached = store.currentVersion(partnerId)) : cached);\n}\n```\nBut partnerId comes from ctx, not the factory. Pass partnerId to the factory: `partnerContextProviders(partnerId, deps)`. Then buildPartnerTurnContext creates providers, and the registered audit provider creates them per call. \n\nSo:\n```ts\nexport function partnerContextProviders(partnerId: string, deps: PartnerContextDeps = {}): InjectionProvider[]\n```\nInternal memo of `current`, `status` (only computed once even if status provider called once anyway). Each provider returns an entry or undefined.\n\n`buildPartnerTurnContext` then:\n```ts\nconst collected = collectInjections(partnerContextProviders(partnerId, deps), {conversationId, partnerId}).message;\n```\nand fields derived by id. \n\nRegistry provider:\n```ts\n{ source: \"partner-context\", provider: (ctx) => ctx.partnerId ? collectInjections(partnerContextProviders(ctx.partnerId), ctx).message : undefined }\n```\nWait, that returns already-collected entries; then audit's outer collect re-collects them (fine, ids preserved, order re-sorted).\n\nOK good. Now what about `soul`? Not an injection (instructions). Keep as-is.\n\n### Guard dedupe decision (final)\n\nAfter the above, partner-context has a guard provider (id `injection_guard`, channel message). Lifecycle has system guard. Both remain → duplicate. To merge: remove partner-context's guard provider. But `buildPartnerTurnContext.guard` field is part of the return contract and tests. If removed, guard=null always and text loses the tail.\n\nI'll now decide: **merge it** — because the task explicitly says to. Implementation: drop the guard provider from partner-context, but keep the `guard` field in the return type for compatibility? A dead field is bad. Update the interface: remove `guard`? Tests use it (partner-context.test asserts guard/text). Removing a field is a larger API change but internal.\n\nHmm, hold on. Let me reconsider the actual duplication risk: the framework dedupe happens per collection. Lifecycle system sections are collected **per section** (one provider each). So even if I keep both, no dedupe occurs. To claim \"框架去重\" I must merge manually.\n\nRisk assessment of merging: If some path uses `buildPartnerTurnContext` **without** installing lifediaryExt sections (e.g., research extension removed lifediaryExt), then removing the guard would lose it. Where is buildPartnerTurnContext called? Only lifecycle beforeRequest (ext installed) and tests. Group member turns use the same harness. OK.\n\nBut is the guard section *truly* applied to partner conversations? `configureTaskChat` removes the extension for tasks. Research chats replace with researchExt (no sections) — but research chats don't call buildPartnerTurnContext. So yes.\n\nWait, one more: the section text is durable `pi.system` only *when the section value changes*. For a partner session, the section exists from the first turn. Yes.\n\nI'll merge, and keep the field `guard` but set it to `null` with a comment? Dead field = bad. Instead remove `guard` from `PartnerTurnContext` and update tests. But \"不做与本需求无关的重构\" — removing 