fect in partner context). It gives the most accurate audit. Only topics has the drain side effect (must avoid) and skills has freeze (avoid). So dryRun only affects those two. Cleaner semantics: \"dryRun = 不做有副作用的渲染（课题 drain、技能冻结）\".\n\nThen partner-context audit shows one row with total length, id \"partner_context\", source \"partner-context\". Good enough; documented.\n\nBut then within `buildPartnerTurnContext` I still want 11 entries for ordering/dedupe. So:\n- Live assembly uses per-segment providers (closure factory).\n- Audit uses one aggregate provider.\n\nThat means the registry's partner source doesn't reflect segments. Acceptable? The audit's purpose is \"这一轮模型看到了什么\" — one aggregate row with total length + id is less informative but honest. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: audit the segments too by using the factory inside the aggregate provider, but `collectInjections` in audit loops per provider and can't return 11 from an aggregate? It can: provider may return `InjectionEntry[]`. So `partnerContextAuditProvider(ctx)` can return the 11 entries from the factory! Then audit lists all 11 (with ids/orders). And live assembly uses the same factory. That's the clean solution:\n\n```ts\n// partner-context.ts\nexport function partnerContextEntries(partnerId, conversationId, deps, current): InjectionEntry[] {\n  // build segments, return entries\n}\nexport function buildPartnerTurnContext(...) {\n  const entries = partnerContextEntries(...);\n  // derive contract/goal/discipline/status/guard from entries by id\n}\nexport const partnerContextProvider: InjectionProvider = (ctx) => {\n  if (!ctx.partnerId) return undefined;\n  const deps = {};\n  const store = partnersStore();\n  const current = store.currentVersion(ctx.partnerId);\n  return partnerContextEntries(ctx.partnerId, Number(ctx.conversationId), {...deps, store}, current);\n};\n```\nAnd registry uses `partnerContextProvider`. In lifecycle, `buildPartnerTurnContext` is called (which internally uses entries). Audit uses the provider. Slight duplication of the \"store/current\" setup but bounded, and `buildPartnerTurnContext` could also call `partnerContextProvider` with a ctx.partnerId! Then:\n\n```ts\nexport function buildPartnerTurnContext(partnerId, conversationId, deps = {}) {\n  const ctx = { conversationId, partnerId };\n  const entries = collectInjections(partnerContextProviders(deps), ctx).message;\n  ...\n}\n```\nwhere `partnerContextProviders(deps)` closes over deps and each provider handles one segment, reading the store lazily. Reading store per provider = multiple reads. To avoid, `partnerContextProviders` can memoize the store reads in a closure (lazy once):\n\n```ts\nfunction partnerContextProviders(deps) {\n  const store = deps.store ?? partnersStore();\n  let cached: PartnerVersion | null | undefined;  // lazy\n  const current = () => (cached === undefined ? (cached = store.currentVersion(partnerId)) : cached);\n}\n```\nBut partnerId comes from ctx, not the factory. Pass partnerId to the factory: `partnerContextProviders(partnerId, deps)`. Then buildPartnerTurnContext creates providers, and the registered audit provider creates them per call. \n\nSo:\n```ts\nexport function partnerContextProviders(partnerId: string, deps: PartnerContextDeps = {}): InjectionProvider[]\n```\nInternal memo of `current`, `status` (only computed once even if status provider called once anyway). Each provider returns an entry or undefined.\n\n`buildPartnerTurnContext` then:\n```ts\nconst collected = collectInjections(partnerContextProviders(partnerId, deps), {conversationId, partnerId}).message;\n```\nand fields derived by id. \n\nRegistry provider:\n```ts\n{ source: \"partner-context\", provider: (ctx) => ctx.partnerId ? collectInjections(partnerContextProviders(ctx.partnerId), ctx).message : undefined }\n```\nWait, that returns already-collected entries; then audit's outer collect re-collects them (fine, ids preserved, order re-sorted).\n\nOK good. Now what about `soul`? Not an injection (instructions). Keep as-is.\n\n### Guard dedupe decision (final)\n\nAfter the above, partner-context has a guard provider (id `injection_guard`, channel message). Lifecycle has system guard. Both remain → duplicate. To merge: remove partner-context's guard provider. But `buildPartnerTurnContext.guard` field is part of the return contract and tests. If removed, guard=null always and text loses the tail.\n\nI'll now decide: **merge it** — because the task explicitly says to. Implementation: drop the guard provider from partner-context, but keep the `guard` field in the return type for compatibility? A dead field is bad. Update the interface: remove `guard`? Tests use it (partner-context.test asserts guard/text). Removing a field is a larger API change but internal.\n\nHmm, hold on. Let me reconsider the actual duplication risk: the framework dedupe happens per collection. Lifecycle system sections are collected **per section** (one provider each). So even if I keep both, no dedupe occurs. To claim \"框架去重\" I must merge manually.\n\nRisk assessment of merging: If some path uses `buildPartnerTurnContext` **without** installing lifediaryExt sections (e.g., research extension removed lifediaryExt), then removing the guard would lose it. Where is buildPartnerTurnContext called? Only lifecycle beforeRequest (ext installed) and tests. Group member turns use the same harness. OK.\n\nBut is the guard section *truly* applied to partner conversations? `configureTaskChat` removes the extension for tasks. Research chats replace with researchExt (no sections) — but research chats don't call buildPartnerTurnContext. So yes.\n\nWait, one more: the section text is durable `pi.system` only *when the section value changes*. For a partner session, the section exists from the first turn. Yes.\n\nI'll merge, and keep the field `guard` but set it to `null` with a comment? Dead field = bad. Instead remove `guard` from `PartnerTurnContext` and update tests. But \"不做与本需求无关的重构\" — removing a field is part of dedupe. Hmm, but the `guard` field is arguably API for tests only. Let me check usages of `.guard`: grep.",
