      {
        "type": "reasoning_text",
        "text": "Now the registry module.\n\n```ts\nimport { guardProvider, type RegisteredInjection } from \"./injection.ts\";\nimport { LIFECYCLE_SECTION_PROVIDERS, ... } from \"./lifecycle-injections.ts\";\n```\nActually registry should list per-source rows. For sections, each provider should be registered separately with source \"lifecycle\" (so audit shows each section's id). For message providers too (source \"lifecycle\" or \"assist\" for memory).\n\nLet me define registry with explicit rows:\n- lifecycle: persona, verification_iron_law, clarify_contract, injection_guard, current_project, skills, topics, skill_slash, recover_note, turn_limit, partner_context (source \"lifecycle\")\n- assist: memory_recall, task_memory\n- partner-context: partnerContextInjectionProvider (one row that expands to many entries)\n\nHmm, but `partner_context` (lifecycle message provider) and `partner-context` module both produce entries — duplicate ids? The lifecycle `partner_context` provider returns ONE entry with id \"partner_context\" containing the whole text, while partner-context's provider returns 11 entries. Both would be registered in audit → for a partner session, audit would show both \"partner_context\" (aggregate) and the 11 segments → duplicate-ish. Bad.\n\nBetter: don't register lifecycle's `partnerContextProvider` in the audit registry; register only the partner-context segment provider. The live path uses partnerContextProvider (aggregate) → framework collects it as one entry. Hmm, then live and audit representations differ.\n\nAlternative: make lifecycle's message collection use `partnerContextSegments`? I.e., in the live path, instead of one aggregate entry, lifecycle adds the partner-context providers directly into the message collection. But partnerContextProvider handles the try/catch, partnerId resolution, turn gate. I could have it return the *segments* array:\n\n```ts\nconst entries = collectInjections(partnerContextProviders(partnerId, conversationId, {workSummary}), ctx).message;\nreturn entries;  // 11 entries\n```\nThen the collected message array contains 11 entries; joining them with \"\\n\\n\" into ONE extra.push message? Original pushed ONE message with the whole turnContext.text. If I now push each entry as a separate message, position/content changes (multiple system messages vs one). Not byte-identical.\n\nSo: keep one aggregate message entry but with id... if id = \"partner_context\", audit can register the same provider (aggregate) → one row. Then don't register partner-context's segment provider. But then requirement 4.2's partner-context source isn't represented... The aggregate entry is produced by `partnerContextProvider` (in lifecycle-injections, source lifecycle) though the content comes from partner-context.\n\nOption: register the aggregate provider under source \"partner-context\" and exclude it from lifecycle rows. But it lives in lifecycle-injections. Source is just a label — I can register `{ source: \"partner-context\", provider: partnerContextProvider }`. That satisfies 4.2 and avoids duplicate rows. But its id is \"partner_context\" (one row, whole length). Fine.\n\nHmm, but then \"11 段\" aren't individually audited. The task only says audit lists \"全部注入条目\" and source distinguishes modules. An aggregate row is defensible... but less impressive.\n\nAlternative: live provider returns the aggregate text but ALSO audit exposes segments — register `partnerContextInjectionProvider` (segments) and not the aggregate. Then live behavior (aggregate) and audit (segments) differ in granularity. That's honest enough: audit shows what's inside the aggregate. I like this better: audit shows 11 rows (contract/goal/.../status), which is more informative, and live pushes 1 message. Document that live collapses them to one message for position parity.\n\nI'll do that: registry has `{source:\"partner-context\", provider: partnerContextInjectionProvider}` and NOT the lifecycle aggregate. And the aggregate entry id \"partner_context\" is not registered → audit won't show a duplicate aggregate.\n\nHmm, but then audit's lengths are per-segment while the live message is one. Fine.\n\nWait, but the dedupe demo: if partner segments include `partner_verification` and lifecycle has `verification_iron_law`, different ids → no dedupe. The dedupe of guard is structural (removed).\n\nOK. Registry:\n\n```ts\nexport const INJECTION_REGISTRY: readonly RegisteredInjection[] = [\n  { source: \"lifecycle\", provider: personaProvider },\n  { source: \"lifecycle\", provider: verificationIronLawProvider },\n  { source: \"lifecycle\", provider: clarifyContractProvider },\n  { source: \"lifecycle\", provider: guardProvider },\n  { source: \"lifecycle\", provider: currentProjectProvider },\n  { source: \"lifecycle\", provider: skillsProvider },\n  { source: \"lifecycle\", provider: topicsProvider },\n  { source: \"lifecycle\", provider: skillSlashProvider },\n  { source: \"lifecycle\", provider: recoverNoteProvider },\n  { source: \"lifecycle\", provider: turnLimitProvider },\n  { source: \"assist\", provider: memoryRecallProvider },\n  { source: \"assist\", provider: taskMemoryProvider },\n  { source: \"partner-context\", provider: partnerContextInjectionProvider },\n];\n```\n\nNote taskMemoryProvider calls taskMemoryBlock which ingests diary / reads DB even during audit! That's a side effect (syncDiaryToMemory writes). Audit should not do that. Make taskMemoryProvider return undefined when ctx.dryRun. Yes.\n\nAlso turnLimitProvider in audit: ctx.turn undefined → no entry.\n\nrecoverNoteProvider: dryRun → undefined already.\n\nskillSlash: ctx.userText undefined → `skillSlashOf(\"\")` → null → undefined.\n\nmemoryRecall: empty text → recallContext returns \"\" (no DB hit? recallContext checks `!memoryEngine || !text.trim()` → returns \"\" before DB). Good.\n\npartner-context in audit: status skipped; contract/goal/discipline read partners.sqlite (read-only).\n\nNow settled.ts: nothing to register. But requirement 4.2 mentions settled as a source. I'll document in registry comment that settled.ts has no prompt injection (only memory ingest/export/observer) — nothing to register. Honest.\n\nNow create injection-registry.ts."
      }
    ],
